[2026-03-14T23:18:52.298Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JH3gNMm8UoCkAztX32R623","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The test expects classes like `status-pending`, `status-confirmed`, etc. The component uses classes like `bg-status-pending/20 text-status-pending`. The regex `/status-pending/` should match `bg-status-pending/20` since it contains \"status-pending\". So the class check should work.\n\nWait, let me re-read the test for badges:\n```\nawait expect(pendingStatus).toHaveClass(/status-pending/)\n```\n\nAnd the actual classes are `inline-block px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold capitalize bg-status-pending/20 text-status-pending`\n\n\nThe regex should definitely match since both `text-status-pending` and `bg-status-pending` contain the pattern. Let me trace through what's actually happening in the test—it creates a pending booking and then tries to find the status element by ID. If `createPendingBooking` is failing or returning undefined, that would explain why the test can't find the element.\n\nI'm realizing this is likely a test isolation issue. Earlier tests that create pending bookings aren't cleaning up after themselves, so when the badges test tries to create a new pending booking for the default date range (2027-08-01 to 2027-08-05), it hits an overlap conflict and gets a 409 error. The response would be an error object without an `id` field, causing the subsequent test assertion to fail.\n\nFor the \"Resetting filter\" test, the same contamination problem means there are more bookings in the system than the test expects. I should either add cleanup hooks between tests or use unique dates for each test to avoid conflicts. Looking at the bookings endpoint, there's already a DELETE mechanism that can reset non-seed bookings, so I might be able to leverage that for cleanup.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cac